<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#AmorphousGrainBoundaryPhase> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the amorphous grain boundary phase used for? — provides magnetic isolation between SmFe12 grains"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#AndersonImpurityModel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The Anderson impurity model describes hybridisation of localised 4f states with conduction electrons in rare-earth PM compounds; it is IN_SCOPE as an electronic-structure model and a GAP because emmo:ElectronicModel has no sub-class for quantum impurity models distinct from DFT or tight-binding approaches."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#AndersonImpurityModel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#AndersonImpurityModel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "anderson impurity model" .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#AndersonImpurityModel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "anderson impurity model"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#AndersonModel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the Anderson model used for? — from which the Kondo model is derived via the Schrieffer-Wolff transformation"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#ClassicalHeisenbergModel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The classical Heisenberg model is an atomistic spin Hamiltonian parameterized by exchange coupling constants Jij used to compute Curie temperature; it is a canonical emmo:AtomisticModel for spin-lattice simulations, and no existing pm: class represents this Hamiltonian model."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#ClassicalHeisenbergModel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#ClassicalHeisenbergModel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "classical heisenberg model" .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#ClassicalHeisenbergModel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "classical heisenberg model"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#CoherentPotentialApproximation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How are electronic and magnetic properties of disordered alloys computed? — effective medium theory used to compute electronic and magnetic properties of disordered alloys by replacing the random potential with an effective coherent potential"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#CrystalFieldTheory> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Crystal field theory is a quantum-mechanical framework computing electrostatic interactions between rare-earth 4f electrons and the surrounding crystal lattice, directly relevant to magnetocrystalline anisotropy in permanent magnets; it is an emmo:ElectronicModel, and no existing pm: class captures this ligand-field approach."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#CrystalFieldTheory> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#CrystalFieldTheory> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "crystal field theory" .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#CrystalFieldTheory> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is crystal field theory computed? — using point charge model to calculate the electrostatic interaction between rare-earth ion 4f electrons and surrounding crystal environment"@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#CrystalFieldTheory> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "crystal field theory"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DepinningCoercivity> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is depinning coercivity used for? — determining coercivity in the depinning-type magnetization reversal mechanism"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DipoleField> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the dipole field computed? — computed via the demagnetization tensor"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DomainNucleation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Domain nucleation is a magnetisation-reversal mechanism central to coercivity modelling in permanent magnets; no existing pm: or magmo: class captures nucleation as a distinct process, requiring a new subclass under pm:MicromagneticsStep."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DomainNucleation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DomainNucleation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "domain nucleation" .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DomainNucleation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "domain nucleation"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DomainWallMotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Domain wall motion is a distinct magnetization-change process simulated in micromagnetics workflows (pm:MicromagneticsStep) but not represented as its own class; a new subclass is needed per the gap criterion."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DomainWallMotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DomainWallMotion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "domain wall motion" .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DomainWallMotion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is domain wall motion used for? — the primary mechanism of magnetization change in materials with well-defined domain structures"@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DomainWallMotion> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "domain wall motion"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DomainWallPropagation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Domain wall propagation is a magnetisation-reversal mechanism (Barkhausen jumps) central to coercivity in permanent magnets and modelled in micromagnetics; no existing pm: or magmo: class captures wall motion as a distinct process."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DomainWallPropagation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DomainWallPropagation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "domain wall propagation" .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DomainWallPropagation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "domain wall propagation"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DynamicalMeanFieldTheory> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Dynamical mean-field theory (DMFT) is a many-body electronic-structure method treating local electron correlations beyond DFT, applied to Sm 4f electrons in permanent magnet calculations; it is a subclass of emmo:ElectronicModel, and no existing pm: class covers correlated-electron many-body methods."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DynamicalMeanFieldTheory> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DynamicalMeanFieldTheory> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "dynamical mean-field theory" .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DynamicalMeanFieldTheory> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is dynamical mean-field theory computed? — applied here with the Hubbard I approximation for Sm 4f electrons and STPF solver for Co 3d electrons"@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DynamicalMeanFieldTheory> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is dynamical mean field theory used for? — combined with first-principles band structure calculations for finite-temperature magnetism"@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#DynamicalMeanFieldTheory> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "dynamical mean-field theory"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#EffectiveAnisotropyField> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the effective anisotropy field used for? — used in the LLG equation to compute resonance frequency"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#FccStructure> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What transformation must fcc structure undergo to acquire hard magnetic properties? — must be transformed to fct for hard magnetic properties"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#FermiDiracSmearing> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How are Kohn-Sham states occupied in DFT calculations? — Fermi-Dirac distribution with width 0.01 eV used to occupy Kohn-Sham states in the DFT calculation"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#ForceTheorem> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the force theorem used for? — used to calculate magnetocrystalline anisotropy energy by comparing band energies along different magnetization directions"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#FullPotentialLinearMuffinTinOrbitalMethod> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the full-potential linear muffin-tin orbital method implemented? — implemented in RSPt"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#FullPotentialLmtoMethod> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is magnetocrystalline anisotropy energy computed using the full-potential LMTO method? — A full-potential linear muffin-tin orbital computational method (RSPt code) used to compute magnetocrystalline anisotropy energy with spin-orbit coupling treated post-SCF"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#GaussianDistributionOfEasyAxes> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What does the Gaussian distribution of easy axes describe? — used to describe the angular spread of grain easy axes in oriented nanocrystalline magnets"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#HeisenbergModelHamiltonian> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The Heisenberg model Hamiltonian is the classical spin Hamiltonian parameterising pairwise exchange interactions between atomic sites in PM spin-dynamics simulations; it is IN_SCOPE and a GAP because pm:SpinDynamicsConfiguration covers solver/configuration settings but not the Hamiltonian functional form as a model class."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#HeisenbergModelHamiltonian> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#HeisenbergModelHamiltonian> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "heisenberg model hamiltonian" .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#HeisenbergModelHamiltonian> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "heisenberg model hamiltonian"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#HubbardIApproximation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The Hubbard-I approximation is a many-body electronic-structure method for treating localised 4f electrons in rare-earth PM compounds; it is IN_SCOPE and a GAP because pm:ExchangeCorrelationFunctional covers DFT XC functionals but not beyond-DFT many-body impurity solvers."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#HubbardIApproximation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#HubbardIApproximation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "hubbard i approximation" .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#HubbardIApproximation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the Hubbard I approximation computed? — neglecting hybridization in the DMFT impurity problem, approximating the self-energy by the atomic self-energy"@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#HubbardIApproximation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "hubbard i approximation"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#HubbardUPotential> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the Hubbard U potential computed? — A phenomenological on-site Coulomb correction term added to DFT to better describe strongly correlated d or f electrons"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#InPlaneMagneticAnisotropy> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is in-plane magnetic anisotropy used for? — which is unfavorable for permanent magnet applications"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#IntersiteExchangeCouplingParameter> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the intersite exchange coupling parameter computed? — calculated via linear-response to small-angle spin fluctuations"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#IsothermalRemanentMagnetization> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is isothermal remanent magnetization used for? — used to construct Henkel plots"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MagneticExchangeConstant> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the magnetic exchange constant computed? — obtained via the Liechtenstein real-space approach using SPR-KKR"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MagneticForceTheorem> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The magnetic force theorem is a first-principles electronic-structure method for computing intersite exchange parameters Jij via second derivatives of total energy w.r.t. spin rotations; it fits under emmo:ElectronicModel as a quantum-mechanical computational approach, and no existing pm: or magmo: class captures this specific perturbative energy-derivative technique."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MagneticForceTheorem> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MagneticForceTheorem> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "magnetic force theorem" .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MagneticForceTheorem> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How are exchange parameters Jij computed using the magnetic force theorem? — computing intersite exchange parameters Jij from the second derivative of the total energy with respect to spin rotations, without performing full self-consistent calculations"@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MagneticForceTheorem> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "magnetic force theorem"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MagneticHyperfineField> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the magnetic hyperfine field measured? — measured by Mössbauer spectroscopy"@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MagneticHyperfineField> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the magnetic hyperfine field used for? — used to estimate local magnetic moments"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MagneticPermeabilityOfVacuum> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the magnetic permeability of vacuum used for? — used in the magnetostatic potential equations for the conical magnet"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MagnetizationReversal> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Magnetization reversal is a core micromagnetic process simulated in pm:MicromagneticsStep workflows but is not itself an existing class; a new subclass under pm:MicromagneticsStep captures the switching process per the gap criterion."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MagnetizationReversal> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MagnetizationReversal> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "magnetization reversal" .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MagnetizationReversal> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "magnetization reversal"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MagnetostaticField> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the magnetostatic field used for? — contributing to spin disorder"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MonteCarloAlgorithm> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the Monte Carlo algorithm used for? — used to calculate equilibrium magnetic properties such as temperature-dependent magnetization by averaging over thermally accessible spin configurations"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MonteCarloStep> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is a Monte Carlo step defined? — corresponding to one attempted spin flip per lattice site on average"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#MuffinTinApproximation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the crystal potential approximated in the muffin-tin approximation? — crystal potential is spherically symmetric within non-overlapping spheres (muffin tins) centered on each atom and constant in the interstitial region"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#NoncollinearMagneticCalculation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is a noncollinear magnetic calculation performed? — A DFT calculation in which spin directions are not constrained to be parallel or antiparallel, allowing treatment of spin canting, spin spirals, and complex magnetic textures"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#QuasiharmonicApproximation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the quasiharmonic approximation computed? — sampling phonon modes at the Gamma point of a supercell, accounting for volume-dependent frequency changes"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#SelfConsistentFieldCalculation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the self-consistent field calculation performed? — Iterative DFT procedure to solve the Kohn-Sham equations self-consistently to obtain the ground-state electron density and magnetic moments"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#StochasticLandauLifshitzGilbert> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the stochastic Landau-Lifshitz-Gilbert method used for? — used in related coercivity studies"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#StochasticLandauLifshitzGilbertEquation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The stochastic LLG equation is an atomistic spin-dynamics equation of motion augmented with a Langevin noise term for thermal fluctuations; it extends the deterministic LLG and belongs under emmo:AtomisticModel, while pm:SpinDynamicsConfiguration could hold its configuration parameters but does not represent the model itself."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#StochasticLandauLifshitzGilbertEquation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#StochasticLandauLifshitzGilbertEquation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "stochastic landau-lifshitz-gilbert equation" .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#StochasticLandauLifshitzGilbertEquation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the stochastic LLG equation used for? — enabling simulation of thermally activated magnetization reversal"@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#StochasticLandauLifshitzGilbertEquation> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "stochastic landau-lifshitz-gilbert equation"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#TemperatureCoefficientOfRemanence> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the temperature coefficient of remanence used for? — a key figure of merit for high-temperature permanent magnet applications"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#TwoSublatticeMeanFieldModel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The two-sublattice mean-field model describes RE-TM magnetic coupling via molecular field coefficients and is directly used to compute temperature-dependent magnetisation in PM materials; it is IN_SCOPE and a GAP because emmo:AtomisticModel has no sub-class for sublattice mean-field exchange models."@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#TwoSublatticeMeanFieldModel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#TwoSublatticeMeanFieldModel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "two-sublattice mean field model" .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#TwoSublatticeMeanFieldModel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the two-sublattice mean field model used for? — A model describing magnetic coupling between rare earth and transition metal sublattices"@en .
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#TwoSublatticeMeanFieldModel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "two-sublattice mean field model"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#TwoSublatticeMicromagneticModel> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the two-sublattice micromagnetic model used for? — to describe the magnetic excitation spectrum including acoustic, optic, and crystal-field modes"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#VacuumPermeability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is vacuum permeability used for? — used as a fundamental constant in the magnetic equations"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#VibratingSampleMagnetometer> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is magnetization measured as a function of applied field and temperature? — An instrument (VSM) used to measure magnetization as a function of applied field and temperature"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#1:5Phase> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the 1:5 phase used for? — used in SmCo5 and PrCo5 permanent magnets"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#EnergyProductDifference(δ(bh)max)> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the energy product difference used for? — used as a measure of magnetic anisotropy"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#FullPotentialLinearizedAugmentedPlaneWave(flapw)Method> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the FLAPW method implemented? — implemented in WIEN2k"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#IdealEnergyProduct(2πms)2> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the ideal energy product used for? — used as a benchmark for comparing experimental energy products"@en .
# 
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale#KkrGreen'sFunctionMethod> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the KKR Green's function method used? — used to solve the Kohn-Sham equations in density functional theory calculations"@en .
